+    /// `TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` never reads at all — so closing straight away makes the
+    /// client's read fail with `ECONNRESET` even though the response bytes were
+    /// already delivered.
+    ///
+    /// What draining buys, precisely: a client that stops writing once it is
+    /// rejected -- every well-behaved one -- has its queue emptied and observes
+    /// the response followed by a clean end of stream, *provided a drain runs at
+    /// all*. It is not an unconditional guarantee, and there are two distinct
+    /// ways to lose it.
+    ///
+    /// One: the drain is bounded by `LINGERING_CLOSE_MAX_BYTES` and
+    /// `LINGERING_CLOSE_TIMEOUT`, because an unbounded one would let a client pin
+    /// a lingering worker for as long as it keeps writing. A client still sending
+    /// when the budget runs out is closed with a non-empty queue and sees the
+    /// reset.
+    ///
+    /// Two: `spawn_bounded_lingering_close` runs at most
+    /// `MAX_LINGERING_CLOSE_WORKERS` drains at once and drops the stream outright
+    /// when they are all busy -- then *no* drain runs and even a client that
